FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] tests/libraries_policy_test.rs:215 — the new refusal probe passes `time.unix(...)` straight into `time.add_days`; `time.unix` answers a number (pinned at tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:1006-1009), while `time.add_days` requires a date (its valid call uses `time.date(...)` at tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs:1040-1044). The program is therefore refused for a type mismatch, not because date arithmetic is unbound, so the assertion is green even though `time.add_days` is implemented and answering. The REPORT's claim that this probe “records that … `time.add_days` remains refused” is false: it would stay green after the gap closes. Fix by probing an actually-open binding, or by asserting the refusal is “unknown binding” rather than “requires a date”.

2. [MINOR] LIBRARIES.md:70 — the new explanatory paragraph and blank lines were inserted between table row 68 and row 75, splitting the bindings Markdown table into two. Rows 75-79 (`files.*`, tensors, linear algebra, statistics, cryptography) no longer render as table rows. Move the paragraph below the completed table.

3. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:2684 — `InvalidByte(position, byte) => char::from(byte)` renders the raw byte as Latin-1. For non-ASCII input, e.g. `base64.decode("é")`, the first byte is `0xC3`, so the error says `invalid byte 'Ã'` instead of naming the character the program wrote. Use the original text at the reported position (`text.as_bytes()`/`text.get(position..)`) for the quoted value, and keep the byte position.

4. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:4042 — `uuid.generate()` calls `uuid::Uuid::new_v4()`, which is the crate's panicking v4 path when `getrandom` fails (and has no clock/entropy source on the wasm playground). Every other I/O/clock failure in this file is deliberately converted to a catchable `Runtime` error; this one can abort the interpreter instead. Use a fallible RNG fill and map the failure to `Error::Runtime`, or document and gate the panic. (Could not execute a RNG failure; this rests on `uuid` 1.x `new_v4` using `getrandom(...).expect(...)`.)

5. [STYLE] tests/encoding_hashing_test.rs:113 — lines 113 and 114 are the identical assertion `hex.decode("4869") == [72, 105]`. The uppercase case is actually covered only by line 115 (`486A`); the duplicate adds nothing and makes the “uppercase” row look broader than it is.

6. [STYLE] tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt:28 — the counts comment is stale after the three new SPEC blocks: it still says “SPEC.md 43 … 61 of 112,” while the file now lists 46 SPEC entries and 64 executable blocks. Update the comment so the bucket cannot be misread as current.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 6
